WEBVTT

00:00:58.380 --> 00:00:58.800
Yes, sir.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:00:59.840
Did you find Tracy?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.520
No, sir. He's still out.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.480
When you find Mr. Tracy, would you ask him if he would condescend to want to take a little repertorial chore for me this afternoon?

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.100
You don't mind?

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.160
Oh, no, sir. I don't mind.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.660
Don't stand there like an imbecile, round him up.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:14.340
Yes, sir.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.650
Well, they're here, Mr. Boswick.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.230
Who's here?

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.040
The students.

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.360
What are they doing here?

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.260
Mr. Boswick, don't you remember?

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.060
It was a promotion tie-up to increase circulation.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.380
And what did I promise them?

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:29.900
Well, they were to work for you for three days.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.840
as real reporters and the one who submits the best story during that time was to receive fifty

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.200
dollars and and a gold medal i might have guessed it i don't know why i let you talk me into these

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.780
things last week you had the place filled with a lot of champion potato pickers and now the place

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.480
is going to be overcrowded with a lot of crazy cubs i won't see mr boswick they're right beside you

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:58.520
oh mr boswick these are the young people you've been so anxious to meet miss nancy drew from the

00:01:58.520 --> 00:01:59.660
the Brinwood School for Young Ladies.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.120
Hiya.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:01.850
Me too.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.730
Miss Phyllis Gimble of the...

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:06.010
Hiya, hiya, hiya.

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:10.050
Oh, Mr. Bostwick, are we really going to have real assignments just like real reporters?

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.410
Why do all of our fans...

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:13.790
Can I cover a scandal?

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.780
Girls, boy, Mr. Bostwick, won't you say a few words to the contestants?

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.850
Yeah, very few.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:25.940
Why, anyone is simple-minded enough to want to work on a newspaper, I've never been able to find out.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.780
Why, Mr. Bostwick, journalism is a very noble and glorious career.

00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:33.570
With all the adventure and romance and everything, I should think you just love it.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.240
I do love it. I love every minute of it.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.310
I love my employees, too. Here, take one.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:42.480
All right, I won't look.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:51.660
What did you get? He's got to write a story about a squirrel in the park.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:52.940
Where's the mayor live?

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:53.400
What?

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.700
I've got to find out how many babies he kissed last week.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.820
A human, it's a story about a goldfish.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.580
Ladies, amateur poetry club.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.730
Go to room 1 p.m.

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.390
All right, now, come on, get going.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.330
All right, children, come on, come on.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.870
And if it isn't news, you needn't bother coming back.

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:09.340
Clever idea, isn't it?

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:10.220
We'll go into that later.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.800
Mr. Brostwick, I don't believe you take us seriously.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.570
My dear Miss Hoosiers, in regard to your supposition,

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.990
I see you're not without perspicacity.

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:23.870
I still haven't been able to find Mr. Tracy.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.910
Every time I depend upon a reporter, I'm double-crossed.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.030
Oh, all right, leave a memo on his desk.

00:03:29.350 --> 00:03:29.970
Tell him to hurry up.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:33.100
the courthouse right away. They're holding an inquest on that Lambert woman.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:36.040
Yes, sir. Yes, sir. In the civil school children.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.920
Are you satisfied with your assignment, Ms. Drew?

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.150
Oh, I think it'll do, for a start.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:16.230
Dr. Carey, please.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:21.690
Raise your right hand.

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.410
Whether the testimony you're about to give will be the truth,

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.630
the whole truth, nothing but the truth, help you God, I do.

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.500
Dr. Carey, as autopsy surgeon,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.080
You examined the body of Kate Lambert?

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:32.900
I did.

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.340
And what did you find?

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.720
That Miss Lambert's death was not caused by heart failure, as Dr. Hibbert certified.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.860
Her death was caused by a poison, sodium farinide.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:45.860
What is sodium farinide?

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.350
It's a chemical used in photography.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.140
Is such a chemical easily obtained?

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.760
Oh, no. Only on special order from the manufacturer.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:55.450
It's a new agent, not yet on the open market.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:59.230
In fact, only those who are well advanced in photography know anything about it.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.060
Miles Lambert.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.180
Mr. Lambert, you were the first to suspect your aunt had not met a natural death?

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:09.990
I was.

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:13.540
What aroused your suspicions?

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:19.510
Well, for years, Dr. Hibbert had assured me that my aunt's heart condition was nothing to worry about.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.840
And when she died so suddenly, I was naturally very shocked and surprised.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:29.980
But even so, I didn't suspect anything until I happened to run across a scientific magazine in my aunt's heart.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.600
library. There was an article in that magazine which had a great deal of significance to me in

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:40.280
view of the circumstances. The article was about sodium Fahrenheit, and the portions of it dealing

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:47.940
with the chemical's poisonous properties had been carefully marked in pencil. Who does this

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:54.050
magazine belong? It's addressed to Eula Denning. Did you mark this article, Miss Denning? Well,

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:59.980
I must have. Why? I keep a file of all chemicals I work with.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.610
with especially poisonous ones and their antidotes.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.380
Then you are interested in photography.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:06.520
Yes.

00:06:07.210 --> 00:06:08.890
How familiar are you with sodium Fahrenheit?

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.920
Well, I know something about it.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Miss Denning, what was your relationship to Kate Lambert?

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.120
My mother was her companion for many years.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.660
After Mother died, Miss Lambert took care of me.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.540
Isn't it true that under the terms of Miss Lambert's will,

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:26.700
you were the sole beneficiary?

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.340
Yes, but...

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:31.360
But I didn't kill her.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.280
Why, Kate Lambert was the best friend I ever had.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:37.490
She put me through school, treated me as her own child.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.430
And yet you coerced her into making a will, leaving all her property to you.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:41.890
No.

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:44.440
A will that was made less than a week before her death.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.290
No, I didn't do it.

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:48.880
I must have taken the poison from the darkroom.

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.030
Mr. Garrett, if you had the chemical tint, that would prove I was innocent.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:53.550
How?

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:56.110
Well, the murderer's fingerprints would be on it.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:57.680
Not if they'd been removed.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:58.960
But they couldn't be removed.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:06:59.960
If any...

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.220
If anyone touched that chemical, the Farinii would etch the prints into the metal.

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.100
A search has been made for the poison container, Miss Denning. It's missing.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:08.780
But it must be in the house somewhere.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.300
Miss Denning, you were the only person connected with the Lambert household...

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.150
with the knowledge of sodium Farinii.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.740
You were the only one who had access to Miss Lambert's room the night she died.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:21.060
And you were the only one who had motive to commit murder.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:23.600
I didn't kill her, I tell you. I didn't. I didn't kill her, I tell you!

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:24.900
That's all.

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:28.480
What's the verdict?

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.960
The Denning girl's being held for...

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:38.820
What's up, baby?

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:40.850
Something we didn't figure on.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.660
I got to get out there and join up on a tin can.

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:04.690
What's all this about a tin can?

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:06.340
There's fingerprints on it.

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:08.650
If the cops find it first, we're going to be in a jam.

00:08:09.020 --> 00:08:10.360
A jam? How?

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:11.680
Never mind how.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.760
If you're at Delia isn't sent up, she'll get all that old lady's dough.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:16.180
Oh, I see.

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:19.580
And if she is sent up, the money goes to Miles Lambert.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:21.000
Not bad.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.270
How much do you get?

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:24.680
Oh, Lambert's take care of me.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:28.150
Looks like I'll have to start being nice to Mr. Lambert.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:29.960
Be nice to nobody but me, Lambert.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.420
me. Bye.

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:37.520
The DA scrolls up this joint, miss.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:39.520
No one can get in here without a port order.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:40.800
I'm here to see to that.

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.760
Oh, well, a man bumped into my fender and ran away and...

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.400
Oh, a hit and runner, eh?

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:47.750
He must be around here someplace.

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:51.920
Well, in that case, miss, you and I'll just take a look around.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.930
Oh, well, maybe you better go on that side of the house,

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:55.870
or I'll go on this one, so he can't escape.

00:09:56.030 --> 00:09:58.870
Good idea. And if you spot him, yell, I'll come a-runner.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:06.900
Hey, what are you doing there?

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.520
Why, I married the gas meter.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.560
Oh, a runnin' hitter, hey?

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:19.060
Yes, for charges, if you pay to have it fixed.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:19.900
Well?

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:24.070
A little squirt.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.030
It'll only cost me three and a half to have my fender fixed.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.880
About 50 cents for the gasoline I wasted coming out here.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.100
It's a dollar back.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:33.160
All right, get along with you.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:37.140
A lot, officer.

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:40.290
He had an awful lot of money for a gas meteorator.

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.200
Maybe I'm in the wrong branch of the service.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:45.420
Did he say he was from the gas company?

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:46.150
Yeah.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.460
Gee, it's kind of funny.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:54.120
Voskrig, all about the Lambert inquest.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:56.930
I was a little delayed on account of a man ran into my center, and I...

00:10:56.930 --> 00:10:57.990
So it was you, huh?

00:10:58.370 --> 00:10:59.970
You switched those assignments, Slip.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.560
I thought reporters always did things like that.

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:04.680
At least they do in the movies.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.280
And besides, it says, write in my textbook on journalism.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:09.480
Then a newspaper man or woman must stop at nothing to get news.

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:11.200
And if she ever intends to impress the editor,

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.740
she must be willing to do much more than just what the assignment calls for.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:14.770
So there.

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:17.640
A thing like this would happen to me.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:20.730
I have it. Every word. Oh, it's sensational.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.290
I bet you 2380 never thought I could do it.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:27.530
Miss Drew, the story of the Lambert inquest was on the street a half hour ago.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:29.680
Possible.

00:11:35.210 --> 00:11:37.710
We were lucky enough to get it from the D.A.'s office.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:48.880
Dear, I was so sure I had a scoop and you'd have to stop the presses.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:49.880
Stop the presses?

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:50.600
Or something.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:55.280
My dear little girlie, will you please take pity on a poor, tired old man?

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:59.240
If another thing like this happens to me today, I'll lose my mind.

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:11.400
Good morning, Pent. Well, how's the star reported this morning?

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.450
Don't mention it. You watch.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.940
I'll show that Mr. Bostitch or whatever his name is.

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.200
I'll get a story yet.

00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:19.200
That's the spirit.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:20.690
Morning, Miss Nancy.

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:21.730
Morning, Nancy.

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.920
Oh, Effie, you're an artist. Boy, oh, boy, what cuisine.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.330
Oh, shucks, Miss Nancy. That's just plain old cereal.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:38.020
I have a favor I want to ask of you.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:39.620
Go right ahead, darling.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.560
I'm in a very receptive mood this morning.

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:42.200
Go right ahead.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:44.680
Well, Eula Denning needs a good lawyer.

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:46.050
I want you to defend her.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:47.430
No, I wouldn't want that case, Nancy.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.000
I've been reading about it and I'm afraid the girl's guilty.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:51.800
There's not one bit of extenuating evidence.

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:53.620
Just the same. I'll bet she didn't do it.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:54.650
What makes you think that?

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.680
I don't know. I guess maybe it's just my woman's intuition.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:12:59.840
But she doesn't look like the type who'd poison anyone.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.000
Can't go by type, Nancy.

00:13:02.076 --> 00:13:03.876
One of the most charming women in history was a murderess.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:06.440
and she committed her crimes for far less than Lambert Estate.

00:13:07.548 --> 00:13:09.208
That's where the man with the funny ear came in.

00:13:09.372 --> 00:13:09.872
Funny you.

00:13:10.812 --> 00:13:11.692
What are you talking about?

00:13:11.996 --> 00:13:13.556
This man sat next to me during the inquest.

00:13:13.756 --> 00:13:16.316
and afterwards got in the car with a woman and ran into my fender.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:19.240
Nancy, I'm going to take that car away from you if you start having accidents.

00:13:19.772 --> 00:13:21.812
But it wasn't my fault. I wasn't even in the car.

00:13:22.076 --> 00:13:24.516
That's why I followed him. I made him pay for that fender, too.

00:13:24.956 --> 00:13:28.076
Guess what, Dad? He went right out to that Lambert estate and tried to get in.

00:13:28.256 --> 00:13:29.216
Probably another reporter.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.500
He didn't look smart enough. He acted awfully suspicious.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.500
Well, I wouldn't concern myself about it too much.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:36.160
You want it?

00:13:36.588 --> 00:13:38.388
Certainly, I'm saving it for last.

00:13:38.628 --> 00:13:38.988
Oh.

00:13:40.268 --> 00:13:40.448
Goodbye.

00:13:40.688 --> 00:13:41.368
Bye-bye, dear.

00:14:11.356 --> 00:14:11.676
garage.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:40.700
What's the matter? What are you hiding for?

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:42.570
Somebody tried to blow up my car.

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:44.970
Aw, stop it, will you? It's nothing to get...

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:58.040
excited over to get excited over say a thing like that right before my very just a whistle bomb

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:03.960
It's just a practical joke?

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.500
Sure. It blows off when you press the starter.

00:15:07.180 --> 00:15:09.360
And boy, did you go into a tailspin.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.840
Ted Nickerson, I'll bet you put that thing in there.

00:15:11.940 --> 00:15:15.240
Oh, don't be an infant. I got better things to do.

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:19.270
Fine. You'll be a menace to civilization.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.710
Anyone who deliberately do a trick like that or...

00:15:21.710 --> 00:15:22.300
Write down. Write down.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.760
Look, they got a load of them.

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:38.800
Perkins, you grew up to be a fiend.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:39.890
What was his idea?

00:15:40.170 --> 00:15:40.750
Insert one.

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:43.430
Nickerson, you ought to be ashamed.

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:46.480
Aren't you going to speak to your sister?

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:48.720
That gangster? She's out of my control.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.140
Aw, you're just jealous because you didn't think of it first.

00:15:51.520 --> 00:15:52.400
Give me those.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:54.000
Hey, those belong to my big brother.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:54.540
They don't.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:58.160
Now, go on. Get out of here before I lose control of myself.

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:03.870
Come in.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:15.400
Nice going, Nancy.

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:17.760
The very idea.

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:20.140
Just a nice quiet morning at the Drews.

00:16:20.450 --> 00:16:21.590
Well, so long. I'll be seeing you.

00:16:21.690 --> 00:16:22.250
Where you going?

00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.280
Now, where do you think I'd be going with this tennis racket?

00:16:25.380 --> 00:16:26.160
To shovel coal?

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:28.100
I'll tell you where he's going.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:29.750
For 10 cents.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.320
I wouldn't pay you a penny. I'm not that interested.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:34.520
Then what are you asking for?

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:35.860
You get in the house.

00:16:37.250 --> 00:16:38.250
I'll tell you anyway.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:42.790
He's got a date with a beautiful girl.

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:46.200
Are you going to play tennis again with that woman?

00:16:46.420 --> 00:16:47.240
What do you mean, woman?

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:49.270
She's only two years older than you are.

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.100
Well, she'd rather play tennis. Why, all right.

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:54.250
But I was sort of depending on you to help me.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:16:57.550
Of course, my getting the best story for the paper isn't really so important.

00:16:58.070 --> 00:16:59.970
But you know how it is. The honor and everything would be.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.320
Oh, it's all right. Go right ahead. Don't mind me.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:06.510
I just... Oh, by the way, I have something for you.

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:08.790
Oh, you have? What is it?

00:17:09.320 --> 00:17:11.460
Nothing, really. Just a little birthday present.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:15.390
Four bucks I had to fix the fender.

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:20.890
Gee, Nancy, thanks. But my birthday isn't for two months yet.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:21.960
That so?

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.780
Well, now that you've seen it, you might as well keep it.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:29.980
Gee, thanks. I don't...

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.480
Wait a minute, Nancy.

00:17:38.570 --> 00:17:41.710
Look, I might postpone my tennis state if you really need me.

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:42.720
Oh, don't bother.

00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.520
I was just going down to the jail to interview Eula Denning

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:47.720
and I wanted you to take your camera along.

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:50.120
So far, she hasn't allowed anyone to take her picture.

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:52.120
Then how do you expect me to get one?

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:53.830
Oh, I'd think of a way.

00:17:55.390 --> 00:17:56.650
Then you don't really need me.

00:17:57.700 --> 00:17:59.970
Well, reporters always take staff photographers.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.720
when they're on important assignments.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:05.390
And besides, Dad doesn't like the idea of my going into a jail alone.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:06.650
I'll go with you.

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.190
A jail is no place for children, Mary Nickerson.

00:18:09.410 --> 00:18:11.050
Oh, let her go. Maybe they'll lock her up.

00:18:11.170 --> 00:18:14.060
I should say not. Now go on, change your clothes and wear your new hat.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:15.350
All right.

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:17.280
I'll get your camera for you, Ted.

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.070
Hurry now. I want to make the noon edition.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:23.220
And besides, maybe you'll have time to fix my fender.

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:26.700
Duked again.

00:18:31.370 --> 00:18:31.930
No cameras.

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:33.800
Well, you see, I...

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.020
You've the lady your camera, Ted.

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:37.300
Huh? Well, okay.

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:39.560
All right. Over there.

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:45.230
What's that?

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.660
I should've known you couldn't get a camera in this place.

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:48.180
I did.

00:18:54.220 --> 00:18:54.840
What was in the case?

00:18:55.310 --> 00:18:57.130
I took out the camera and put in a block of wood.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:58.690
Figured on something like this.

00:18:59.240 --> 00:18:59.640
Now, look.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.560
You don't expect me to take a picture in here after what she said.

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:03.480
Well, silly.

00:19:03.940 --> 00:19:05.640
And if they catch us, we'll stay here.

00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:06.900
Oh, stop your worry.

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:08.510
Put your hat on.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:10.780
Inside?

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:11.830
Of course.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:14.270
You never saw a real newspaper man without his hat on, did you?

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:16.100
How are they even eating them?

00:19:18.570 --> 00:19:19.760
No, no, not what they have.

00:19:20.210 --> 00:19:20.950
It looks awful.

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:24.200
For God's sakes, what are you doing?

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:25.260
You just gave it to me.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:25.720
No, no, no.

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:26.480
Now, let me think.

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:36.880
Fix it is right.

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:41.620
Why, you ruined it.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:51.400
There, now you look like a real reporter, not a store window dummy.

00:20:01.180 --> 00:20:06.060
Yes. Miss Denning, I'm Nancy Drew of the Tribune, and this is Mr. Nickerson, my photographer.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:09.360
Oh, a reporter. You seem so young.

00:20:09.850 --> 00:20:12.850
I guess I'd better explain. We're not real reporters.

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:16.180
I just want to win a prize in journalism, and that's why I wanted an interview.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:18.350
What did you say your name was?

00:20:18.700 --> 00:20:22.200
Nancy Drew. Are you related to Carson Drew, the attorney?

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:23.580
He's her father.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:28.720
Oh, I see. Miss Denning, do you know a man with a funny ear?

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:29.970
It's also...

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.690
Oh, I don't. Why do you ask?

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:37.160
Well, there was one at the inquest, and I was wondering if he was a friend of yours.

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:38.710
I don't know who he could be.

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:41.120
Miss Denning, I don't think you did anything.

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:44.810
That is, I mean, I think you're innocent.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:48.130
It's all right with you. I'm going to ask my father to come and see you.

00:20:48.540 --> 00:20:49.660
Oh, if he only would.

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:54.030
You see, there's... well, there's so much evidence against me.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:56.800
Why, it's even worse now than it was yesterday.

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:58.380
Did they find something else?

00:20:59.300 --> 00:20:59.980
Police checked with...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:04.060
the manufacturers and learned the only sale of sodium faranite made in this part of the country

00:21:04.060 --> 00:21:12.400
was to me uh-oh gee if you only had that tin can the one the poison came in you know you told about

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:17.500
the inquest yesterday it's disappeared someone probably stole it i saw it just beyond the day

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:23.840
when i was cleaning out the dark room i understand why they didn't find it it was what's the matter

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:29.980
tin was empty i didn't throw it away i wanted to save it because the man

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.520
The manufacturer's address was on it.

00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:34.790
Gee, think hard. Maybe you can remember what you did with it.

00:21:35.020 --> 00:21:39.070
I put some empty bottles and things in the basement in an old cupboard.

00:21:41.330 --> 00:21:42.730
That container must have been with them.

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:44.230
Maybe it's still there.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:44.630
Yeah.

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.620
You just leave it to me. And please don't tell anyone.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.030
Oh, I almost forgot. Miss Denning, may we take a picture of you?

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:52.360
I don't mind.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:53.380
Hurry up, Ted.

00:21:55.250 --> 00:21:58.300
Thanks a lot, Miss Denning, and we'll let you know what happened.

00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:07.430
A little bit to your right.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:08.520
Look sad now.

00:22:08.870 --> 00:22:09.230
Hold it.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:15.480
Big idea.

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:16.560
What's going on out here?

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:18.820
We're taking the quadrofoam to port them with the foreign family.

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:19.940
Amscray, amscray.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:20.960
Is the gentleman with the prisoner?

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:22.460
I think we'd better be leaving.

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:39.200
Well, what if you can't get in? It's still illegal.

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:40.400
Not for a reporter.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.380
A reporter has the right to do things an ordinary person shouldn't.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:44.140
Ah, fool.

00:22:44.360 --> 00:22:47.000
Now, go ahead and do just exactly as I told you.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:47.730
Okay.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.400
But if anything happens, don't blame me.

00:22:57.620 --> 00:22:57.840
Hello?

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:03.540
You don't want to subscribe to any magazines, do you?

00:23:03.940 --> 00:23:04.200
No.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:05.830
I didn't think you did.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:09.220
Are you watching this place?

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:09.990
Yeah.

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:17.160
Well, I guess you get kind of lonesome around here all by yourself, huh?

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:18.120
Sometimes.

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:21.450
I'm not very busy right now.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:25.090
If you like, I could play a little game of rummy or something.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:27.940
Say, that's an idea, son.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:29.190
Drop a chance.

00:23:29.250 --> 00:23:29.690
Wow.

00:23:33.710 --> 00:23:36.330
Uh, look, do you mind if I sit over there?

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:38.130
I'm kind of superstitious.

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:39.980
Oh, sure, sure.

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:50.390
We ought to have something to shoot at, son.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.450
Say, uh, penny a point?

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:53.210
A penny a point?

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:55.820
Yeah, that'll be fine.

00:23:57.750 --> 00:23:59.220
I'll keep score for you.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.380
Don't do that.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:18.340
It puts me off of my game.

00:24:19.300 --> 00:24:20.260
I'm sorry.

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:59.910
shall be gone.

00:26:01.532 --> 00:26:02.372
How do we stand?

00:26:03.068 --> 00:26:04.008
I'll figure it out.

00:26:10.556 --> 00:26:11.816
got me in a fine mess.

00:26:12.668 --> 00:26:14.768
I lost my whole week's allowance to that rummy shark.

00:26:15.036 --> 00:26:15.876
Well, it was worth it.

00:26:16.028 --> 00:26:17.068
I found the tin can.

00:26:17.208 --> 00:26:17.368
Yeah?

00:26:17.724 --> 00:26:18.564
Uh-huh, and...

00:26:18.876 --> 00:26:20.416
did that man with a funny ear was there.

00:26:20.924 --> 00:26:22.004
chase me out of the basement.

00:26:22.428 --> 00:26:23.088
Gosh, Nancy.

00:26:23.548 --> 00:26:24.728
We took an awful chance.

00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:27.820
Maybe we shouldn't have done it.

00:26:28.092 --> 00:26:29.772
We'd better get this tin right down to the police station.

00:26:30.028 --> 00:26:32.028
and see if Captain Tweedy can find any fingerprints on it.

00:26:54.156 --> 00:26:55.356
Catch a look at the time.

00:26:55.660 --> 00:26:56.860
My tennis date was at three.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.480
Oh, but this is much more important than tennis.

00:26:59.052 --> 00:26:59.972
If I pass her up this time,

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.100
She'll never play with me again.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:02.080
Don't you want to go to the PlayStation?

00:27:02.440 --> 00:27:03.060
You don't need me.

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:04.180
You can tell me about it later.

00:27:04.440 --> 00:27:04.660
Goodbye.

00:27:06.044 --> 00:27:06.884
Whee!

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:05.220
Well, why do you stay now? Come on, get away. Break it up.

00:28:12.270 --> 00:28:14.400
Hello, Pat. Glad you're home.

00:28:14.660 --> 00:28:18.520
Yeah, huh? You're doing up so late. You can get back to sleep.

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:20.020
Good night.

00:28:22.230 --> 00:28:22.710
Good sleep.

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:23.570
Come on in.

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:28.580
No. Come on in. Tell me all about it.

00:28:33.160 --> 00:28:36.860
Dad, you've always taught me to do right, haven't you?

00:28:37.310 --> 00:28:38.170
I've tried to, Nancy.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:42.260
Dad, if I believed in something I knew was right,

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:43.900
I should always live up to it.

00:28:44.170 --> 00:28:46.300
More than that. Don't back down an inch.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:51.500
You made a promise who was right. Bet you you'd keep it.

00:28:51.730 --> 00:28:55.210
Certainly. A person who fails to live up to his promise is a week.

00:28:56.060 --> 00:28:58.060
And you'd never want me to break a promise, would you?

00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.360
Of course not.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:05.460
If you make a promise in good faith, I'd expect you to live up to it, no matter what it cost you.

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:07.480
You'd help me live up to it?

00:29:07.740 --> 00:29:09.850
Certainly. What kind of a father do you think I am?

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:13.720
Well, then, you're going to defend Eula Denning because I promised her you would.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:14.610
What?

00:29:15.460 --> 00:29:17.620
Not that. You just said you'd help me keep a promise.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:21.740
Nancy, you tricked me into this. You're always doing that to me. Now, I won't have...

00:29:23.750 --> 00:29:24.450
Well, all right.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:29.400
If you can give me any reason to believe that Eula Denning is innocent, I'll take her case.

00:29:34.380 --> 00:29:38.060
When I went out to that Lambert house this afternoon, I sneaked in and found that tin of sodium faranine.

00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:40.340
The one that Miss Denning claims could have had fingerprints on it.

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:41.540
You two went out there alone?

00:29:42.370 --> 00:29:46.050
And I know it was important evidence because that man with a funny ear chased me out of the basement.

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:48.250
Dad, that's not the worst of it.

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:51.970
When I was taking that tin down to the police, a woman stole it from me.

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:54.030
So that proves other people are mixed up in it.

00:29:54.130 --> 00:29:55.870
You should have gone to the police in the first place.

00:29:55.970 --> 00:29:59.880
Oh, I know, Ed. Everything's gone wrong. I've failed all around.

00:30:00.410 --> 00:30:01.550
And I'm just not intelligent.

00:30:01.790 --> 00:30:02.310
That's right.

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:05.810
I mean, darling, everybody makes mistakes.

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:14.830
It's my fault, because I was foolish enough,

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:16.670
the only evidence that could maybe save her.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:17.870
I'm responsible.

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:19.410
Well, don't worry, Nancy.

00:30:19.670 --> 00:30:21.410
I'll see Eula Denning first thing in the morning.

00:30:21.890 --> 00:30:25.040
Oh, Dad, honestly, I feel 10 years younger.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:26.780
Don't be too enthusiastic.

00:30:27.390 --> 00:30:28.930
So far, there isn't a stick of evidence

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:29.850
we could take into court.

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:32.440
There will be, if you can get hold of that man with a funny ear.

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:34.160
You think you'd know him again if you saw him?

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:35.880
With that ear? I'll say.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:37.580
It looked like one of Effie's popovers.

00:30:37.620 --> 00:30:38.760
Oh, cauliflower ear.

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:39.400
What?

00:30:39.620 --> 00:30:41.000
I thought a cauliflower went away.

00:30:41.060 --> 00:30:41.840
What did you say?

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:43.440
I can't understand you, Nancy.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:47.680
Well, how can you expect you splashing around like a seal after a fish?

00:30:47.840 --> 00:30:49.380
That's no way to talk to your father.

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:50.580
Oh, dear, they're talking!

00:30:50.880 --> 00:30:52.580
Oh, why don't you take them out of here?

00:30:52.620 --> 00:30:53.520
Here, here's the towel.

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:56.060
Oh, dear, now I'll have to start all over again.

00:30:56.340 --> 00:30:59.820
I said that that man's ear looked like one of Effie's popovers.

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:02.220
I said it was probably a cauliflower ear.

00:31:02.850 --> 00:31:05.090
That might indicate that he was a boxer or a wrestler.

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:09.650
What connection such a man could have with this case is more than I can see.

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.890
Dad, I felt all along that that man with a funny ear was a clue.

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:15.160
I think you ought to do something about him.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:17.830
I'm going to do something about a girl with a funny face.

00:31:18.200 --> 00:31:19.200
Come on, baby, you're going to bed.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:20.480
Oh, no, baby.

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:22.780
You'll always be your daddy's baby.

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:24.540
I will be.

00:31:25.490 --> 00:31:26.850
Everybody loves a baby.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:29.770
That's why I'm in love with you, pretty baby.

00:31:32.840 --> 00:31:47.350
good night baby good night baby good night baby I'm going to leave you now in the morning the

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:51.120
morning the bright and early morning the morning

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:04.650
Charm, I'm sure. Draw up a chair.

00:32:07.510 --> 00:32:09.270
No, thank you. I've been standing all day.

00:32:09.920 --> 00:32:12.440
Do you keep pictures of all kinds of prize photos and people like that?

00:32:12.980 --> 00:32:14.600
You should meet some of my wife's folks.

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:17.440
But you came to the right place, sister. I know them all personally.

00:32:18.040 --> 00:32:18.980
What's your guy look like?

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:22.290
Oh, you'll know him in a minute. He has a cauliflower ear.

00:32:33.100 --> 00:32:36.160
Here, if you're recognizing, let me know.

00:33:06.140 --> 00:33:07.080
What do you want?

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:11.410
Excuse me a second, Alan.

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:19.400
How did you find me here?

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:21.920
Oh, I get it.

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:24.020
Where would one expect to find you these days?

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.750
Quiet, small fry.

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:29.430
She's not so pretty.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:31.810
Look, what's on your mind?

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:33.170
Look at this clipping I found.

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:37.490
Soxie Anthins.

00:33:37.550 --> 00:33:37.930
Who's that?

00:33:38.120 --> 00:33:39.600
He's the man I saw at the Lambert plays.

00:33:40.050 --> 00:33:40.830
He used to be a boxer.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:43.090
And right now he's at Maxie's gymnasium on First Street.

00:33:43.230 --> 00:33:43.730
How do you know?

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:44.730
Yeah, how do you know?

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:48.320
Well, I just asked myself where I'd go if I were a prize fighter

00:33:48.320 --> 00:33:49.860
and decided it would be to a gymnasium.

00:33:50.020 --> 00:33:53.320
So I called up all the places in town and pretended I was Mr. Anthins' girlfriend.

00:33:53.680 --> 00:33:55.240
Pretty soon I found out he was at Maxie's.

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:55.980
Then I hung up.

00:33:56.750 --> 00:33:56.930
Yeah.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:33:59.480
Well, it was nice of you to tell me about it.

00:33:59.540 --> 00:33:59.820
I...

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:00.950
I got to get back.

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:02.150
Goodbye, big shot.

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:03.930
Certainly, I don't want to keep you.

00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:06.360
I just thought I'd let you know where I'll be in case Dad asks you.

00:34:06.750 --> 00:34:08.070
You're not going down to that gym alone.

00:34:08.460 --> 00:34:09.200
Of course I am.

00:34:09.380 --> 00:34:11.060
Something has to be done about Soxie right away.

00:34:11.320 --> 00:34:12.360
He's important evidence.

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:14.280
So you came over here to tell me about it,

